        `,simpleExplanation:"Smart shopping is careful shopping.",example:"You may make a list before buying groceries.",analogy:"Smart shopping is like packing a backpack with only needed things.",reflectionQuestion:"What is one smart shopping habit?",options:["Making a list","Buying everything","Buying without thinking","Ignoring prices"],correctAnswer:"Making a list",term:"Smart Shopping"}]},{id:"module-4",title:"Module 4",subtitle:"Investing Basics",description:"This module introduces the idea of investing and how it may help money grow over time. It begins by explaining why people invest: to make their savings work harder than they would by simply keeping it in a safe place. You will learn what stocks are, how they represent small pieces of a company, and how their value can change. The module also covers mutual funds, which pool money from many people so a professional can invest in different assets together. It teaches about risk and how some investments offer bigger rewards but can also be less certain, so it is important to choose carefully. The final lesson shows how long-term growth uses time to help investments increase, especially when decisions are patient and steady. This module gives a gentle introduction to investing without pressure, helping you understand the basics of growing money responsibly.",lessons:[{id:"lesson-16",title:"Why Invest?",summary:"Investing helps money grow over time.",explanation:`
